<!--
The 2:3 card, and the one piece the redesign repeats most: the library grid and the three rails on
Home all show it, so it is a view of its own rather than four templates that would drift apart.

Its size is two tokens, PosterCardWidth x PosterCardHeight, exactly 2:3 as declared, and who sets
them depends on where the card is. Home's rails take them as declared: the chosen cover size. The
library grid overrides both on its own surface, width to automatic and height to one and a half
times the width its cell leaves the cover, so its cards stretch to fill the row the way the
prototype's minmax(148px, 1fr) tracks do - nine covers of about 147 at 1600 px. The chip, the tick
and the words keep their size when a cover stretches, as they do in the prototype; that is why the
height comes from the grid and not from a Viewbox, which would scale them with it.

This used to say a card could not stretch because "Avalonia has no aspect ratio", and that was
false, found on 2026-09-11 when the owner asked for the documentation to be consulted: there is no
property like CSS's aspect-ratio, but the height can be set from the width, and here the grid does.

There is no artwork here and there is not going to be in 0.2.0: the application ships with no TMDB
token, so the initials are not a placeholder waiting for a picture, they are what the card shows.
They are marked Raw for automation because the card is already announced by its title - a reader
that heard "EF, El Faro de Piedra" would be hearing the same title twice, once spelled out.

It carries no accessible name of its own: in the library the button around it is named by the title,
and a name here would be a second announcement of the same card.
-->
<UserControl
    x:Class="ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Library.PosterCardView"
    xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:automation="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Automation;assembly=Avalonia.Controls"
    xmlns:converters="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Data.Converters;assembly=Avalonia.Base"
    xmlns:home="clr-namespace:ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Home"
    xmlns:library="clr-namespace:ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Library"
    Width="{DynamicResource PosterCardWidth}"
    x:DataType="library:IPosterCard">
  <UserControl.Resources>
    <home:ResourceKeyConverter x:Key="CardWord" />
    <!--
      Its own width, and not the detail cards'. A poster decoded at the provider's 780 costs 3,65 MB
      of pixels where the same picture at this card's 148 costs 131 KB — twenty-eight times less,
      because what is paid for is area — and a grid asks for a screenful at once where a detail view
      asks for one.

      The cache is larger because a screenful is not one card. The library's grid virtualises by row
      and keeps about thirty-six cards alive, measured in LibraryView.axaml's own comment; Courses'
      WrapPanel keeps all of them. Eight entries against either would decode and drop the same
      pictures on every pass, so this holds a screenful and the scroll either side of it: about
      17 MB full, at this width.

      Since 2026-09-11 the library's covers stretch past 148 - 147 to 156 at 1500 and 1600 px, and
      up to about 187 at the narrowest window - so a picture there is drawn a little larger than it
      was decoded. That is registered with its numbers and not decided here: following the fluid
      width would decode every picture again on each resize, because the cache keys on the width.
    -->
    <library:CachedPosterConverter
        x:Key="GridPoster"
        Kept="128"
        Width="148" />
  </UserControl.Resources>
  <UserControl.Styles>
    <!--
      The same card, in a rail: the chip keeps its glyph and drops its word. The prototype writes the
      word only in the library grid, where a card has the width for it, and gives the rail's smaller
      covers the icon alone — a chip that says «Película» across a third of a thumbnail is a label
      competing with the picture under it. Nothing is lost to a reader: the word is announced Raw,
      and the button around the card already says which kind it is.
    -->
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.glyph-chip TextBlock#KindWord">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      The shape in the chip: a strip of film, or a screen for a series. Two setters and a class
      rather than a converter, because a converter for this has to ask the application for a
      resource by name and both of its "not found" arms are branches nothing can take.
    -->
    <Style Selector="Path.kind-glyph">
      <Setter Property="Data" Value="{DynamicResource IconFilm}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Path.kind-glyph.show">
      <Setter Property="Data" Value="{DynamicResource IconShow}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      THE CARD AS THE RAILS DRAW IT, which is where these numbers have always been. They are setters
      and not attributes because the library's tile overrides three of them, and an attribute is a
      local value that no style can reach past.
    -->
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView StackPanel#CardStack">
      <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="{DynamicResource DensityGutter}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView StackPanel#CoverCaption">
      <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="{DynamicResource Space8}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView TextBlock#CardMeta,
                     library|PosterCardView TextBlock#CardStatus,
                     library|PosterCardView StackPanel#CardEpisodes > TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeCaption}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      THE CARD AS THE LIBRARY'S GRID DRAWS IT — the prototype's tile, measured in its own capture on
      2026-09-12 and written here line by line: 10 px from the cover to the title, and three lines
      stacked with nothing between them, whose line boxes are 1.5 times their size and whose third
      one starts 3 px lower (design/AP Reelume.dc.html:307 and :316-320).

      The rails keep what they had, and that is not laziness: the prototype draws ITS rails
      differently too — a gap of 8, a title at 13 and a subtitle at 11, with no status line at all -
      so the tile's numbers would be the wrong numbers there.

      The layout rounding goes off on the caption, and that is the whole reason the rhythm was 5 px
      out before this. Avalonia rounds each control's desired size UP to a whole pixel, so line
      boxes of 20,25 and 17,25 measured 21 and 18 and the three lines took 60 px where the prototype
      stacks them in 57,75. With the rounding off they stack exactly as the browser stacks them, and
      the text still lands on whole pixels because Skia snaps a baseline on its own.
    -->
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.grid-tile StackPanel#CardStack">
      <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="10" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.grid-tile StackPanel#CoverCaption">
      <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="UseLayoutRounding" Value="False" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.grid-tile StackPanel#CoverCaption :is(TextBlock)">
      <Setter Property="UseLayoutRounding" Value="False" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.grid-tile TextBlock#CardTitle">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13.5" />
      <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="20.25" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.grid-tile TextBlock#CardMeta,
                     library|PosterCardView.grid-tile TextBlock#CardStatus,
                     library|PosterCardView.grid-tile StackPanel#CardEpisodes > TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11.5" />
      <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="17.25" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="library|PosterCardView.grid-tile TextBlock#CardStatus,
                     library|PosterCardView.grid-tile StackPanel#CardEpisodes">
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,3,0,0" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>
  <!--
    What separates the cover from the words under it: the density gutter in a rail, and the
    prototype's fixed 10 in the library's grid, which is a gap its own density never changes. Both
    live in the styles above.
  -->
  <StackPanel x:Name="CardStack">
    <!--
    The hairline and not the control border: this rectangle separates a card from the one beside it
    and is not something to press, so it is the 1.8:1 brush the token split was made for.
    -->
    <Border
        Height="{DynamicResource PosterCardHeight}"
        Background="{DynamicResource ControlFillBrush}"
        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource ShellHairlineBrush}"
        BorderThickness="1"
        BoxShadow="{DynamicResource ElevationShadow}"
        ClipToBounds="True"
        CornerRadius="{DynamicResource PosterCornerRadius}">
      <Panel>
        <!--
        The prototype's cover, which is not a picture: a two-stop gradient from the title's hue, a
        radial glow in the top left corner, a diagonal hatch and a ring. All four of its layers since
        2026-08-24; the hatch was the missing one, and a repeating LinearGradientBrush draws it.

        PosterArtOpacity is 0 in the two high contrasts, where the card falls back to the fill and
        the initials it has always had: a hue picked by a hash is a contrast ratio nobody decided.
        -->
        <!--
        The artwork, in a panel of its own since 2026-09-12 so that the chip can read it: what the
        chip blurs is a brush of THIS panel, and a brush of the whole cover would be a brush of the
        chip itself.
        -->
        <Panel x:Name="CoverArt">
        <library:PosterArtView x:Name="PosterArtSurface" Title="{Binding Title}" />
        <!--
          The cover, when there is one. Until 2026-09-04 there was nowhere here to put it: the
          application downloaded posters from the provider and let somebody pick their own, stored
          them, backed them up — and this grid, which is the screen the library is looked at through,
          drew the generated gradient over initials for every title it had.

          UniformToFill and not Uniform: the card is 2:3 and so is a provider's poster, so the two
          agree; a personal cover is whatever shape somebody's file was, and filling is what keeps
          the grid a grid rather than a row of different heights. The Border above already clips.
        -->
        <Image
            x:Name="PosterPicture"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasPoster}"
            Source="{Binding PosterFile, Converter={StaticResource GridPoster}}"
            Stretch="UniformToFill"
            automation:AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />
        <!--
          The initials stand in for artwork, so they go when there is artwork. They stay underneath a
          picture that fails to decode, which is what the detail cards already do.
        -->
        <TextBlock
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            FontSize="{DynamicResource FontSizeDisplay}"
            FontWeight="SemiBold"
            Foreground="{DynamicResource PosterInitialsBrush}"
            IsVisible="{Binding !HasPoster}"
            Text="{Binding Initials}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />
        </Panel>
        <!--
        The kind chip, top left of the cover, which is where the prototype puts it and what it puts
        there: one word on a dark tint, over the artwork rather than under it. Announced Raw - the
        button around this card already says the title, and a reader that heard "Film, El Faro de
        Piedra, 2019, not started" would be hearing the shelf read out along with the book.

        THE TINT IS A CHILD AND SO IS WHAT IS BEHIND IT, blurred: the prototype's chip carries
        backdrop-filter: blur(8px), and Avalonia has no backdrop filter at all — its BlurEffect
        blurs the element's own layer and its acrylic only tints, both read in its source on
        2026-09-12. What it does have is a brush of another visual, and here the backdrop is the
        card's own artwork, which this card owns: a brush of CoverArt, the chip's own box taken out
        of it, mirrored outwards the way the filter specification says a backdrop blur mirrors, and
        blurred at a radius that Skia turns into the same sigma the 8 px of CSS asks for.

        The rects are written by the code behind because only it knows how wide the word makes the
        chip, and the bleed is three sigma: what is blurred is a box of the artwork larger than the
        chip on every side, and the pill clips it back.
        -->
        <Border
            x:Name="KindChip"
            Margin="8"
            HorizontalAlignment="Left"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Classes="poster-chip"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasKind}"
            SizeChanged="OnKindChipSizeChanged">
          <Panel>
            <Border x:Name="ChipBackdrop">
              <Border.Effect>
                <BlurEffect Radius="26" />
              </Border.Effect>
            </Border>
            <Border Background="{DynamicResource PosterChipSurfaceBrush}" />
            <StackPanel x:Name="KindWords" Orientation="Horizontal">
              <Path
                  Classes="icon size-12 kind-glyph"
                  Classes.show="{Binding IsShow}"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Stroke="{DynamicResource PosterChipInkBrush}" />
              <TextBlock
                  x:Name="KindWord"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Text="{Binding KindKey, Converter={StaticResource CardWord}}"
                  automation:AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Panel>
        </Border>
        <!--
        An unreachable medium veils the cover it stands for and says so across its foot, which is
        what the prototype paints at :311. It was a pill in the corner until 2026-09-12 — before
        that, a pill UNDER the card, where it pushed that card's title below the titles beside it.
        It is the same control the detail cards and the episode row mount, in the form a cover asks
        for: the veil lives inside UnavailableBadge, so there is still one place that says this.

        Its place in the stack is the prototype's too: over the chip, which it dims, and under the
        rule, which goes on saying how far in you are.
        -->
        <library:UnavailableBadge Classes="cover-veil" />
        <!--
        At the foot of the artwork rather than under the title: it is a rule across the poster, not a
        control to read. Its three numbers and its two colours live in poster-track, next to the
        other two bars this application draws, with what each of them cost to find written there.
        -->
        <ProgressBar
            Classes="poster-track"
            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasKnownProgress}"
            Maximum="1"
            Value="{Binding CompletedFraction}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource HomeResumeProgressLabel}" />
        <!--
        And a tick, top right, for something seen to the end: 20 across at 6 px from the cover's
        inside, which is 7 from its outer edge, with a 13 px check — the prototype's numbers, at
        :313. It drew 22 at 8 until 2026-09-12, when the two were counted in pixels side by side.

        It is absent, not dimmed, when the medium is out of reach, which is the prototype's own
        condition (done: … && av, at :2416). The veil owns the cover then, and a tick above it would
        be the card saying two things at once about the same title.
        -->
        <Border
            Width="20"
            Height="20"
            Margin="6"
            HorizontalAlignment="Right"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Background="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
            CornerRadius="{DynamicResource CornerRadiusPill}">
          <Border.IsVisible>
            <MultiBinding Converter="{x:Static converters:BoolConverters.And}">
              <Binding Path="IsWatched" />
              <Binding Path="IsAvailable" />
            </MultiBinding>
          </Border.IsVisible>
          <Path
              Classes="icon size-13"
              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Stroke="{DynamicResource AccentTextBrush}"
              Data="{DynamicResource IconCheck}"
              automation:AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />
        </Border>
      </Panel>
    </Border>
    <!--
    Everything under the cover, in one panel, because the Appearance page can turn all of it off:
    the prototype's «Mostrar títulos bajo las portadas» leaves a wall of artwork and nothing else.
    Nothing is lost to a reader when it goes — the button around the card carries the title as its
    accessible name, which is where the full one has always lived.
    -->
    <StackPanel
        x:Name="CoverCaption"
        IsVisible="{DynamicResource CoverTitlesVisible}">
    <!--
    One line and no more, ended by an ellipsis: a second line pushed the caption of one card below
    the caption of the card beside it, so a row of cards read as a ragged edge instead of a grid.
    The full title is still announced - the button around the card carries it as its name.
    -->
    <TextBlock
        x:Name="CardTitle"
        FontWeight="SemiBold"
        Text="{Binding Title}"
        TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
        TextWrapping="NoWrap" />
    <!--
    The prototype's two lines under the title, and they are two rather than one because they answer
    different questions: what this is - "2024 - 111 min - Suspense" - and where you are in it. The
    second is a status for a film and a count for a series, never both.
    -->
    <TextBlock
        x:Name="CardMeta"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}"
        IsVisible="{Binding HasMeta}"
        Text="{Binding MetaText}"
        TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
        TextWrapping="NoWrap" />
    <TextBlock
        x:Name="CardStatus"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}"
        IsVisible="{Binding !CountsEpisodes}"
        Text="{Binding StatusKey, Converter={StaticResource CardWord}}"
        TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
        TextWrapping="NoWrap" />
    <StackPanel
        x:Name="CardEpisodes"
        Orientation="Horizontal"
        Spacing="{DynamicResource Space4}"
        IsVisible="{Binding CountsEpisodes}">
      <TextBlock
          Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}"
          Text="{Binding EpisodeCountText}" />
      <TextBlock
          Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}"
          Text="{DynamicResource CatalogEpisodesSuffix}" />
    </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
